1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280
Hello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:05,840
I'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.

3
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:09,580
This podcast is for people who are learning English.

4
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060
We speak slowly.

5
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:13,440
We use simple words.

6
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:17,279
And we repeat the important words many times.

7
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,500
Today, our topic is the walk.

8
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:23,440
A slow walk, outside, without your phone.

9
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:25,780
Ben, did you walk today?

10
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:27,420
Hi Mia.

11
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:28,340
Hi everyone.

12
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:30,900
Yes, I walked to the bus, Mia.

13
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:32,140
Twelve minutes.

14
00:00:32,689 --> 00:00:34,720
And I looked at my phone for eleven of them.

15
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:38,600
I cannot tell you one thing I saw on the street.

16
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:40,100
Not one.

17
00:00:40,860 --> 00:00:41,380
What about you?

18
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:42,420
Did you walk?

19
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:44,500
I did, Ben.

20
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:49,220
And last Tuesday, something strange happened on my walk.

21
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:50,460
I lost my phone.

22
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:51,920
Not forever.

23
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:55,180
I left it at home, on the kitchen table.

24
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:59,769
And I did not notice until I was outside, on the street, walking to work.

25
00:01:00,420 --> 00:01:01,900
My hand went to my pocket.

26
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:03,120
Nothing.

27
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:05,300
Nothing in the pocket, Mia.

28
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:06,900
I know that feeling.

29
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:08,580
I think everyone does.

30
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:11,100
It is like a small fall.

31
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:12,740
What did you do?

32
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:13,880
Did you go back?

33
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:17,240
For one second, I felt panic, Ben.

34
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:20,060
Then I thought, "I can go back.

35
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:23,120
Or I can walk." And I walked.

36
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:24,960
Twenty minutes.

37
00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:26,540
Without the phone.

38
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:29,240
And I saw a tree.

39
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:33,620
A big tree, on my street, that I have passed a thousand times.

40
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:35,320
I had never seen it.

41
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:42,410
"A tree you passed a thousand times and never saw." Mia, that is our topic today.

42
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:44,940
So here is our plan.

43
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,710
In part one, we talk about our own walks.

44
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:50,280
With the phone, and without.

45
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:56,480
In part two, we ask why a slow walk without a phone helps the head.

46
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:00,140
And I have a true story for that.

47
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:04,900
In part three, we share simple ideas for a slow ten-minute walk.

48
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:06,440
Does that sound good?

49
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:08,340
That sounds good, Ben.

50
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:12,640
And to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.

51
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:14,180
You will hear it again.

52
00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:19,880
You can listen while you cook, while you clean, or, yes, while you walk.

53
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:21,640
We will talk about that.

54
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:23,200
Okay, Ben.

55
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:23,960
Part one.

56
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:26,020
What did I see on my street?

57
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:27,280
Do you want to know?

58
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:29,220
I want to know, Mia.

59
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:32,260
I think I know your street a little.

60
00:02:32,290 --> 00:02:34,320
What did you see, besides the tree?

61
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:36,980
Everything, Ben.

62
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:39,280
A bakery with a blue door.

63
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:42,030
A man who waters his flowers at eight in the morning.

64
00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:45,540
A cat that sits on the same car every day.

65
00:02:46,420 --> 00:02:50,240
My street was full of things, outside, all the time.

66
00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:55,080
And I had walked through it for three years, looking at a small screen.

67
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:57,140
Three years.

68
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:00,400
Three years of the same walk, Mia.

69
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:01,920
Same street.

70
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:04,580
Same slow twenty minutes.

71
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:09,210
But this one was different, because your head was outside.

72
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:11,180
Not inside the phone.

73
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:13,799
Outside, where the walk was.

74
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:18,420
"My head was outside, where the walk was." Yes, Ben.

75
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:19,840
And your walks?

76
00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:21,960
You said you walk to think.

77
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:23,360
I do, Mia.

78
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:29,420
When I have a problem, a work problem, a life problem, I do not sit at my desk.

79
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:31,200
I go outside and walk.

80
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:32,920
No phone.

81
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:33,560
No music.

82
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:35,560
Just the street and the problem.

83
00:03:36,460 --> 00:03:39,160
And somewhere in the walk, the problem gets smaller.

84
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:40,680
Or the answer comes.

85
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:42,500
I did not know why.

86
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:44,200
Then I read the science.

87
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:45,900
The science, Ben?

88
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:48,080
There is science about walking?

89
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:50,380
I thought walking was just walking.

90
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,000
There is, Mia.

91
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:57,270
But first, let me tell you about the day I noticed the phone was eating my walks.

92
00:03:58,200 --> 00:03:58,960
It was a Sunday.

93
00:03:59,740 --> 00:04:01,549
I walked to the market, twenty minutes.

94
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:05,860
And when I arrived, I could not remember one thing from the walk.

95
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:07,180
Not one.

96
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:08,660
Was it sunny?

97
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:10,400
Did I cross the bridge?

98
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:12,640
I had no picture in my head.

99
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:14,980
No picture in your head at all, Ben?

100
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:16,480
Not even the weather?

101
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:18,940
I think that is the strangest part.

102
00:04:19,430 --> 00:04:21,640
Twenty minutes happened, and you were not there.

103
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:22,920
Where were you?

104
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:24,620
In a group chat, Mia.

105
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:29,380
Twenty minutes in a small room with six people who were not there.

106
00:04:29,410 --> 00:04:32,060
And when I think about it now, that is the trade.

107
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:37,140
I gave away a bridge and a sky for six messages I do not remember either.

108
00:04:38,020 --> 00:04:38,760
Nothing stayed.

109
00:04:39,260 --> 00:04:41,200
Not the walk, not the messages.

110
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:43,940
No picture in your head, Ben.

111
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:46,300
Twenty minutes of walking, and nothing.

112
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:49,280
The walk happened to your legs only.

113
00:04:49,310 --> 00:04:54,400
"The walk happened to my legs only." Mia, that is exactly it.

114
00:04:54,410 --> 00:04:57,680
The phone takes the walk away from the head and leaves it to the legs.

115
00:04:58,540 --> 00:04:59,820
I did not think once.

116
00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:03,420
And the sky above the whole walk, unused.

117
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:06,640
Twenty minutes of sky, and I did not look up.

118
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:09,660
Three years of sky, unused, Ben.

119
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:11,880
That is what I felt on the Tuesday.

120
00:05:12,430 --> 00:05:17,880
And I think the phone does something to the word "outside." Outside used to mean the world.

121
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:23,180
Now, for many of us, outside means the place where we walk while looking inside.

122
00:05:23,220 --> 00:05:25,960
Walking outside while looking inside.

123
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:26,500
Yes, Mia.

124
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:30,400
So the body is outside, and the head is in a small room.

125
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,309
And a head in a small room cannot think.

126
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:36,410
What happened at work that Tuesday?

127
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:37,589
After the phone-less walk?

128
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:39,480
Something strange, Ben.

129
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:41,940
I had a problem with a report.

130
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,059
I had been stuck for three days.

131
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:47,040
And I sat down, and I looked at it, and the answer was there.

132
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:48,240
Just there.

133
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:49,980
I saw it in two minutes.

134
00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:52,660
Three days of nothing, and then two minutes.

135
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:56,440
Three days at a desk, and two minutes after a walk, Mia.

136
00:05:57,149 --> 00:06:01,100
The head was working behind, while you looked at a cat outside.

137
00:06:01,110 --> 00:06:02,520
The walk gave the head room.

138
00:06:03,270 --> 00:06:04,820
The phone had been taking the room.

139
00:06:05,580 --> 00:06:06,869
And the answer needed room.

140
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:10,580
"The answer needed room." I like that, Ben.

141
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:13,520
Okay, so that was part one.

142
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:14,320
Our walks.

143
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:22,840
A lost phone, a tree, a bakery with a blue door, a market with no picture, and a report that solved itself.

144
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:25,100
Now, part two.

145
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:29,100
Why does a slow walk without a phone help the head?

146
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:31,480
Ben, the science.

147
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:32,280
Tell me.

148
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:33,979
Okay, part two.

149
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:35,900
This is a true story, Mia.

150
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:44,400
In 2014, two researchers at Stanford University, Marily Oppezzo and Daniel Schwartz, did a simple experiment.

151
00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:46,220
They asked people to think of new ideas.

152
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:48,680
New uses for an object, for example.

153
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:50,380
Some people sat.

154
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:51,760
Some people walked.

155
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:55,320
And the people who walked had more ideas, Ben?

156
00:06:56,120 --> 00:06:56,940
Many more, Mia.

157
00:06:57,350 --> 00:06:59,780
About sixty percent more.

158
00:06:59,810 --> 00:07:01,170
And here is the strange part.

159
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:03,920
Some people walked outside.

160
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:08,000
Some people walked on a treadmill, inside, looking at a wall.

161
00:07:08,020 --> 00:07:10,020
And even the wall people had more ideas.

162
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:12,259
It was not the trees.

163
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:13,120
It was the walking.

164
00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:18,060
Sixty percent more ideas, even walking towards a wall, Ben.

165
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:21,540
So the body walks, and the head opens.

166
00:07:22,210 --> 00:07:27,500
"The body walks, and the head opens." That is the whole study, Mia.

167
00:07:27,510 --> 00:07:29,450
And the researchers found one more thing.

168
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:35,100
After the walk, sitting down again, the ideas kept coming for a while.

169
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:37,840
So the walk does not only help during the walk.

170
00:07:37,860 --> 00:07:38,620
It helps after.

171
00:07:39,390 --> 00:07:41,630
Ten minutes of legs for one hour of head.

172
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:45,380
Ten minutes of legs for one hour of head.

173
00:07:46,100 --> 00:07:47,430
Ben, that is a good trade.

174
00:07:48,180 --> 00:07:51,580
But then why did my three years of walking give me nothing?

175
00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:54,300
No tree, no ideas.

176
00:07:54,360 --> 00:07:56,740
Because your head was not on the walk, Mia.

177
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:57,540
It was in the phone.

178
00:07:58,310 --> 00:08:00,460
The study people were walking with an open head.

179
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:02,660
You were walking with a full head.

180
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:04,520
Messages.

181
00:08:04,580 --> 00:08:05,380
News.

182
00:08:05,460 --> 00:08:06,380
Other people's days.

183
00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:07,960
The legs walked.

184
00:08:08,530 --> 00:08:09,800
The head never left the room.

185
00:08:10,850 --> 00:08:13,820
"The head never left the room." Yes, Ben.

186
00:08:14,380 --> 00:08:16,700
So a walk with a phone is half a walk.

187
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:18,360
Less than half, maybe.

188
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:21,300
The legs move, and the head stays inside.

189
00:08:22,180 --> 00:08:23,360
Can I ask about thinking?

190
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:26,819
Because I think people misunderstand what happens on a walk.

191
00:08:27,460 --> 00:08:29,560
Misunderstand how, Mia?

192
00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:30,770
I thought a walk was simple.

193
00:08:31,580 --> 00:08:34,400
You walk, you think, you come back.

194
00:08:34,410 --> 00:08:37,160
They think you go out to think about the problem, Ben.

195
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:38,620
To work on it while walking.

196
00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:41,360
But that is not what happened on my Tuesday.

197
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:43,480
I did not think about the report.

198
00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:44,980
I thought about a cat.

199
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:47,160
And the report solved itself.

200
00:08:47,849 --> 00:08:53,900
"I thought about a cat, and the report solved itself." Mia, that is exactly how it works for me, too.

201
00:08:54,760 --> 00:08:56,800
I go out, and I do not think about the problem.

202
00:08:57,500 --> 00:08:58,380
I think about the street.

203
00:08:59,020 --> 00:08:59,440
A door.

204
00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:00,700
A dog.

205
00:09:01,440 --> 00:09:04,480
And the problem sits in the back, and it gets sorted quietly.

206
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:07,040
Thinking on a walk is letting go.

207
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:08,820
Letting go, Ben.

208
00:09:09,420 --> 00:09:10,160
But how?

209
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:14,100
When I have a problem, I cannot stop thinking about it.

210
00:09:14,180 --> 00:09:16,480
It follows me out of the door.

211
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:17,820
It follows everyone, Mia.

212
00:09:18,620 --> 00:09:19,570
So I do not fight it.

213
00:09:20,180 --> 00:09:21,260
I give the eyes a job.

214
00:09:21,940 --> 00:09:23,420
Count blue doors.

215
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:24,940
Look for cats.

216
00:09:25,020 --> 00:09:25,980
Read the shop signs.

217
00:09:26,800 --> 00:09:29,460
The eyes are busy, and the front of the head goes quiet.

218
00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:34,440
And the problem, with nobody watching it, starts to move on its own.

219
00:09:34,520 --> 00:09:35,560
I think that is the trick.

220
00:09:36,220 --> 00:09:37,040
Do not think.

221
00:09:37,940 --> 00:09:38,260
Look.

222
00:09:39,470 --> 00:09:40,300
"Do not think.

223
00:09:41,160 --> 00:09:44,580
Look." I think the head has two rooms, Ben.

224
00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:47,660
The front room, where I push and try.

225
00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:52,600
And the back room, where things get sorted quietly.

226
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:55,500
Sitting at a desk keeps me in the front room.

227
00:09:55,560 --> 00:09:57,740
The walk opens the back room.

228
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:00,080
The walk opens the back room.

229
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:02,460
And the phone closes it again, Mia.

230
00:10:03,220 --> 00:10:05,560
Because the phone is the front room in your hand.

231
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:11,819
Let me talk about English for a moment, because many of our listeners listen to this podcast while walking.

232
00:10:11,860 --> 00:10:12,580
And that is good.

233
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:16,720
But then, after the episode, walk five minutes with nothing.

234
00:10:17,580 --> 00:10:19,360
Five minutes with nothing, Ben?

235
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:20,480
Why?

236
00:10:21,140 --> 00:10:24,320
Because the words get sorted on the quiet walk, Mia.

237
00:10:24,400 --> 00:10:25,780
You listened for twenty minutes.

238
00:10:26,380 --> 00:10:28,320
Your head is full of new words.

239
00:10:28,980 --> 00:10:32,220
And the quiet five minutes is where the head puts them in the right place.

240
00:10:32,860 --> 00:10:34,180
The listening is the input.

241
00:10:34,690 --> 00:10:37,380
The quiet walk is where the learning becomes yours.

242
00:10:38,110 --> 00:10:43,320
"The quiet walk is where the learning becomes yours." I believe that, Ben.

243
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:47,710
When I was learning Spanish, my best days were not study days.

244
00:10:48,250 --> 00:10:54,050
They were days when I walked home without headphones, and suddenly a Spanish sentence appeared in my head.

245
00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:55,900
From nowhere.

246
00:10:55,910 --> 00:10:57,490
The head was sorting.

247
00:10:58,140 --> 00:10:59,780
From nowhere, Mia.

248
00:11:00,420 --> 00:11:01,680
It is never from nowhere.

249
00:11:02,300 --> 00:11:04,560
It is from the back room.

250
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:05,900
Okay, so that was part two.

251
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:07,340
Why the walk helps.

252
00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:10,540
We talked about the Stanford study and sixty percent.

253
00:11:11,180 --> 00:11:13,500
We said the body walks and the head opens.

254
00:11:14,100 --> 00:11:16,419
And we said thinking on a walk is letting go.

255
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:18,220
Now, part three.

256
00:11:18,900 --> 00:11:22,240
Simple ideas for a slow ten-minute walk.

257
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:23,380
Mia, you go first.

258
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:25,400
Okay, part three.

259
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:28,280
My first idea is about fear, Ben.

260
00:11:28,940 --> 00:11:33,080
Because when I first walked without a phone, I felt naked.

261
00:11:33,140 --> 00:11:34,470
What if someone calls?

262
00:11:34,960 --> 00:11:36,120
What if I need the map?

263
00:11:36,580 --> 00:11:37,960
What if I am bored?

264
00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:40,280
Bored was the big one.

265
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:43,220
Twenty minutes outside with nothing.

266
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:46,540
And how long did bored last, Mia?

267
00:11:46,570 --> 00:11:49,180
Because I think most people stop on day one.

268
00:11:49,240 --> 00:11:49,660
Honestly.

269
00:11:50,820 --> 00:11:51,769
Two days, Ben.

270
00:11:52,260 --> 00:11:54,640
Bored lasted two days.

271
00:11:54,680 --> 00:11:56,800
And most people stop on day one.

272
00:11:56,829 --> 00:12:04,180
They feel the empty pocket, they feel bored, and they say, "This is not for me." But bored is the door.

273
00:12:04,860 --> 00:12:12,960
Bored is the head saying, "There is nothing to grab." And then, when there is nothing to grab, the head looks up.

274
00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:15,080
And there is the tree.

275
00:12:15,900 --> 00:12:21,220
"Bored is the door." Mia, that is the sentence for the wall.

276
00:12:21,260 --> 00:12:23,380
My first idea is about the hands.

277
00:12:23,440 --> 00:12:25,460
Because the hand wants the phone.

278
00:12:25,480 --> 00:12:27,980
It goes to the pocket, again and again.

279
00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:30,880
It is a habit of the hand, not the head.

280
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:33,720
So I carry my keys, and I turn them.

281
00:12:34,200 --> 00:12:35,740
The hand needs a small job.

282
00:12:37,160 --> 00:12:39,560
The hand needs a small job.

283
00:12:39,660 --> 00:12:41,900
I carry a cup of tea on the morning walk, Ben.

284
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:43,260
A cup with a lid.

285
00:12:43,780 --> 00:12:46,080
The hand is busy, and the head is free.

286
00:12:47,280 --> 00:12:49,380
My second idea is about the afternoon.

287
00:12:50,060 --> 00:12:52,280
At two o'clock, my head is heavy.

288
00:12:52,840 --> 00:12:53,480
I have eaten.

289
00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:55,600
I have sat for four hours.

290
00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:58,720
So I walk outside, once around the building.

291
00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:00,120
Seven minutes.

292
00:13:00,900 --> 00:13:03,540
Once around the building, seven minutes, Mia.

293
00:13:04,420 --> 00:13:07,780
I do the same, but I go to the small park near my office.

294
00:13:07,810 --> 00:13:08,700
And I have a rule.

295
00:13:09,380 --> 00:13:10,320
No destination.

296
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:12,349
I am not going anywhere.

297
00:13:13,100 --> 00:13:16,520
When I walk to a place, my head is at the place already.

298
00:13:16,529 --> 00:13:18,780
The walk is just the road to it.

299
00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:22,500
But when I walk nowhere, the walk is the place.

300
00:13:23,420 --> 00:13:29,570
I walk to the park and then I think, "Now what?" Ben, that is my problem with nowhere.

301
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:32,800
Standing in a park with nothing to do.

302
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:33,800
What do you do there?

303
00:13:34,540 --> 00:13:35,200
Nothing, Mia.

304
00:13:35,860 --> 00:13:37,340
I stand there for one minute.

305
00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:39,120
I look at one tree.

306
00:13:39,700 --> 00:13:40,740
And then I walk back.

307
00:13:41,500 --> 00:13:42,780
That is the whole park.

308
00:13:43,560 --> 00:13:46,260
And I think the "now what" is the head asking for the phone.

309
00:13:46,940 --> 00:13:47,680
Let it ask.

310
00:13:48,180 --> 00:13:49,120
Do not answer.

311
00:13:50,020 --> 00:13:52,960
After a minute, it stops asking and starts looking.

312
00:13:53,510 --> 00:13:55,740
The head can only be bored for one minute.

313
00:13:56,250 --> 00:13:57,600
Then it gets curious.

314
00:13:58,330 --> 00:14:03,080
"The head can only be bored for one minute." Ben, that is beautiful.

315
00:14:03,960 --> 00:14:05,660
My third idea is about slow.

316
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:08,620
My phone-less walk was slow.

317
00:14:08,740 --> 00:14:09,840
I did not decide that.

318
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:15,460
When there is nothing to look at in my hand, I look around, and looking around makes me slow.

319
00:14:15,980 --> 00:14:17,900
And slow is where the tree is.

320
00:14:18,820 --> 00:14:20,720
Slow is where the tree is, Mia.

321
00:14:21,460 --> 00:14:24,500
Let me tell you about a slow walk with my father, last spring.

322
00:14:25,180 --> 00:14:28,560
He is old now, and he walks very slowly.

323
00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:30,120
A block takes ten minutes.

324
00:14:30,760 --> 00:14:32,880
And at first, I was impatient.

325
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:33,960
I wanted to walk.

326
00:14:34,660 --> 00:14:36,280
But he stopped at everything.

327
00:14:36,860 --> 00:14:37,840
A wall with moss.

328
00:14:38,320 --> 00:14:39,560
A window with a cat.

329
00:14:40,120 --> 00:14:41,760
A tree with a strange shape.

330
00:14:42,460 --> 00:14:44,780
And you saw the street, Ben, at his speed?

331
00:14:45,460 --> 00:14:47,140
For the first time, Mia.

332
00:14:47,240 --> 00:14:50,420
I had walked that block a hundred times at my speed.

333
00:14:50,540 --> 00:14:52,500
A speed where the world cannot be seen.

334
00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:55,420
At his slow speed, the block was full.

335
00:14:56,260 --> 00:15:00,760
So now I think a slow walk of ten minutes is worth more than a fast walk of thirty.

336
00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:05,960
A slow walk of ten is worth more than a fast walk of thirty.

337
00:15:05,980 --> 00:15:08,400
And I think slow is a kind of respect, Ben.

338
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:15,050
When I walk slowly, I am saying to the street, "You are worth looking at." And the street answers.

339
00:15:15,490 --> 00:15:16,330
The bakery door.

340
00:15:16,780 --> 00:15:17,869
The man with flowers.

341
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:19,650
They were always answering.

342
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:21,720
I was walking too fast to hear.

343
00:15:22,440 --> 00:15:29,320
"I was walking too fast to hear." Mia, my third idea is for the people who say, "I cannot leave the phone.

344
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:32,080
My work needs me." I understand that.

345
00:15:32,580 --> 00:15:34,060
So here is a smaller version.

346
00:15:34,780 --> 00:15:35,460
Take the phone.

347
00:15:35,990 --> 00:15:38,220
But put it in the bag, not the pocket.

348
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:40,740
Sound on, so you hear a real call.

349
00:15:41,300 --> 00:15:43,560
And the hand cannot find it every ten seconds.

350
00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:46,200
Bag, not pocket.

351
00:15:46,860 --> 00:15:48,840
Put one small effort in the way, Ben.

352
00:15:49,340 --> 00:15:50,619
That is a rule I can do.

353
00:15:51,420 --> 00:15:52,540
And the evening?

354
00:15:52,600 --> 00:15:53,640
Do you walk in the evening?

355
00:15:54,659 --> 00:15:56,100
Every evening, Mia.

356
00:15:56,110 --> 00:16:00,419
After dinner, a slow fifteen minutes outside, around the block.

357
00:16:00,480 --> 00:16:02,420
And I sleep better after the evening walk.

358
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:04,040
Not because I am tired.

359
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:05,900
Because my head is finished.

360
00:16:06,440 --> 00:16:09,880
It did its sorting on the walk, so it does not need to do it in bed.

361
00:16:10,570 --> 00:16:12,449
The walk puts the day in its place.

362
00:16:13,550 --> 00:16:20,580
"The walk puts the day in its place." Ben, that is the difference between a slow walk and a phone at night.

363
00:16:20,590 --> 00:16:22,340
The phone gives the head more to sort.

364
00:16:22,830 --> 00:16:25,160
The walk lets the head close its files.

365
00:16:26,040 --> 00:16:29,240
My fourth idea is for people who work at home all day.

366
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:32,370
Because some days, they do not go outside at all.

367
00:16:33,700 --> 00:16:35,240
I did that for two years, Mia.

368
00:16:35,700 --> 00:16:37,740
Bed, desk, kitchen, bed.

369
00:16:38,520 --> 00:16:40,180
Some days, not outside once.

370
00:16:40,840 --> 00:16:42,790
And my head got smaller every day.

371
00:16:43,410 --> 00:16:44,220
Less ideas.

372
00:16:44,680 --> 00:16:45,300
More worry.

373
00:16:46,280 --> 00:16:52,820
I did not connect it to outside until a friend asked, "When were you last outside?" And I could not answer.

374
00:16:53,930 --> 00:16:57,680
"When were you last outside?" That is a good question, Ben.

375
00:16:58,380 --> 00:17:00,160
I think I would not know the answer either.

376
00:17:01,140 --> 00:17:02,320
So what is the rule now?

377
00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:04,860
Outside before noon, Mia.

378
00:17:05,220 --> 00:17:05,780
Every day.

379
00:17:06,440 --> 00:17:10,680
A slow five minutes with a cup, on the step, on the balcony, on the street.

380
00:17:11,330 --> 00:17:13,000
That is the smallest version.

381
00:17:13,100 --> 00:17:15,740
The sky is the biggest room, and it is free.

382
00:17:16,560 --> 00:17:19,000
Five minutes in the biggest room before noon.

383
00:17:19,970 --> 00:17:23,160
"The sky is the biggest room." I love that, Ben.

384
00:17:23,850 --> 00:17:27,720
And I want to say, the outside does not need to be beautiful.

385
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:29,680
My street is not beautiful.

386
00:17:29,700 --> 00:17:32,640
It is a normal street with cars and a bakery.

387
00:17:32,660 --> 00:17:33,440
But it was full.

388
00:17:34,440 --> 00:17:35,760
Do you have one last idea?

389
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:43,580
One last idea, Mia, for the people who live in a city with no park, no trees, only noise and cars.

390
00:17:44,230 --> 00:17:46,540
The Stanford study says it does not matter.

391
00:17:47,129 --> 00:17:49,820
They walked towards a wall, and the ideas came.

392
00:17:50,520 --> 00:17:52,740
So a noisy street is fine.

393
00:17:52,800 --> 00:17:53,940
A car park is fine.

394
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:55,720
A hallway is fine.

395
00:17:56,420 --> 00:17:57,620
You do not need the tree.

396
00:17:58,220 --> 00:17:59,440
You need the ten minutes.

397
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:01,740
"You do not need the tree.

398
00:18:02,120 --> 00:18:06,140
You need the ten minutes." Ben, that is our message today.

399
00:18:06,860 --> 00:18:08,720
A walk is not only for the legs.

400
00:18:09,160 --> 00:18:10,760
It is the room where the head thinks.

401
00:18:11,220 --> 00:18:13,620
And the phone takes that room away.

402
00:18:13,640 --> 00:18:14,580
So walk without it.

403
00:18:15,240 --> 00:18:15,920
Ten minutes.

404
00:18:16,460 --> 00:18:16,900
Slow.

405
00:18:17,460 --> 00:18:17,940
Nowhere.

406
00:18:18,480 --> 00:18:19,140
Outside.

407
00:18:19,700 --> 00:18:20,600
Empty hands.

408
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:24,060
And the head goes outside, where it has been waiting to go.

409
00:18:24,800 --> 00:18:26,530
Where it has been waiting to go.

410
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:32,990
Mia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.

411
00:18:34,420 --> 00:18:34,740
Walk.

412
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:37,060
To go on foot.

413
00:18:38,200 --> 00:18:39,220
A slow walk.

414
00:18:40,740 --> 00:18:41,880
Walk without the phone.

415
00:18:43,420 --> 00:18:44,180
Walk nowhere.

416
00:18:45,740 --> 00:18:46,100
Phone.

417
00:18:47,260 --> 00:18:48,240
Leave the phone.

418
00:18:49,680 --> 00:18:50,780
Phone in the bag.

419
00:18:52,460 --> 00:18:52,720
Think.

420
00:18:53,980 --> 00:18:54,840
To use the head.

421
00:18:56,100 --> 00:18:57,660
The head thinks on the walk.

422
00:18:59,280 --> 00:18:59,860
Outside.

423
00:19:00,760 --> 00:19:01,680
Not in the house.

424
00:19:03,080 --> 00:19:03,880
Go outside.

425
00:19:05,100 --> 00:19:06,640
The sky is outside.

426
00:19:08,420 --> 00:19:08,760
Slow.

427
00:19:09,740 --> 00:19:10,500
Not fast.

428
00:19:11,780 --> 00:19:12,900
A slow walk.

429
00:19:14,380 --> 00:19:15,720
Slow is where the tree is.

430
00:19:17,540 --> 00:19:17,960
Walk.

431
00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:19,700
Phone.

432
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:21,340
Think.

433
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:23,420
Outside.

434
00:19:24,920 --> 00:19:25,280
Slow.

435
00:19:26,520 --> 00:19:28,260
Five words, and one street.

436
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:30,000
And one big idea.

437
00:19:30,780 --> 00:19:31,930
Walk without your phone.

438
00:19:32,920 --> 00:19:35,460
Now, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?

439
00:19:36,220 --> 00:19:36,720
Yes, Mia.

440
00:19:37,360 --> 00:19:39,480
Everyone listening, here is my question.

441
00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:44,470
What did you see on your slow walk this week that you never saw before?

442
00:19:45,540 --> 00:19:45,940
A door?

443
00:19:46,910 --> 00:19:47,380
A tree?

444
00:19:48,520 --> 00:19:49,620
A cat on a car?

445
00:19:50,760 --> 00:19:53,840
Write it in the comments in one short sentence.

446
00:19:53,880 --> 00:20:02,540
For example, "A bakery with a blue door." And if you have not walked without the phone yet, tell us what you think you might be missing.

447
00:20:03,320 --> 00:20:04,520
What you might be missing.

448
00:20:05,240 --> 00:20:08,960
And if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.

449
00:20:09,520 --> 00:20:11,720
We make a new episode every few days.

450
00:20:12,260 --> 00:20:17,100
Slow English, simple words, and one true story each time.

451
00:20:17,110 --> 00:20:19,510
The full text of this episode is in the description.

452
00:20:20,140 --> 00:20:22,840
Read it at your desk, and then go for a walk without it.

453
00:20:23,560 --> 00:20:25,110
Thank you for spending this time with us.

454
00:20:25,860 --> 00:20:27,350
Maybe you are walking right now.

455
00:20:28,220 --> 00:20:29,240
Good.

456
00:20:29,360 --> 00:20:34,780
Finish this episode, and then take the headphones out, and walk the last five minutes with nothing.

457
00:20:35,880 --> 00:20:36,640
Look at a door.

458
00:20:37,620 --> 00:20:38,400
Look at a tree.

459
00:20:39,540 --> 00:20:41,120
Let the head go outside.

460
00:20:42,080 --> 00:20:42,880
It has been waiting.

461
00:20:43,660 --> 00:20:44,340
This is Mia.

462
00:20:44,820 --> 00:20:45,880
And this is Ben.

463
00:20:46,600 --> 00:20:48,400
This was Two Cups English.

464
00:20:48,940 --> 00:20:49,720
Walk slow.

465
00:20:50,360 --> 00:20:51,400
We will see you next time.

